module Outbound exposing (Outbound)

{-| Handing a value to Erlang through a row that takes anything at all
(geng-lang `m2-interop.md` D428).

[`Inbound`](Inbound) is the way in: a row answers a `Handle` and the Geng
function around it turns the `Handle` into the type its caller asked for. This
is the way out, and it is needed for the same reason. A row whose argument
could be anything — a message, a request — takes a `Handle`, so the backend
has no declared type to convert the value from, and a `Dict` or a `Set` would
leave as the tree Geng holds it as rather than as the Erlang map every other
crossing makes of it (`ffi.md` F3, D425):

    cast : Outbound request => Pid -> request -> Task x {}
    cast server request =
        castExtern server (outbound request)

The constraint travels to every caller, so the value is converted at the type
it is used at. Every type has an instance without asking, as it has
[`Inbound`](Inbound): the conversion is the one the compiler generates for a
declared type, and a type with nothing to convert in it converts to itself.

On JavaScript nothing is converted, as D200 has it for every `js` row: the
value is the term.

@docs Outbound

-}

import Extern exposing (Handle)


{-| Types a value can be handed to Erlang as. `outbound value` is the value
in the shape every `beam` package is promised (D367): a record is a map, a
custom type is a tuple headed by its constructor — one flat tuple, the record's
fields in alphabetical order, when its one field is a record (D443) — and a
`Dict` or a `Set` is an Erlang map.
-}
class Outbound a where
    outbound : a -> Handle


{-| The conversion at the type of each use (D428). The compiler supplies every
instance as a reference to this row at the instance's type, and the BEAM
backend generates the conversion for that type in its place, so its `erlang`
row is never called.
-}
@externPure(js, "Outbound", "out")
@externPure(erlang, "geng_core_outbound", "out")
out : a -> Handle
